  ["A 62-year-old presents with severe pain of the upper left teeth for three days with no dental cause found, followed by a vesicular rash on the left cheek and palate that stops sharply at the midline. The most likely diagnosis is:","Herpes zoster of the maxillary division of the trigeminal nerve",["Acute apical abscess of 2.6","Primary herpetic gingivostomatitis","Trigeminal neuralgia"],"Unilateral dermatomal vesicles with a prodrome of pain are shingles; the midline cut-off is the clue. Trigeminal neuralgia causes brief shocks without a rash."],
  ["A white, corrugated patch on the lateral border of the tongue of a 35-year-old that cannot be wiped off is most associated with:","Epstein-Barr virus and HIV infection",["Candida albicans and denture wearing","Human papillomavirus type 16","Herpes simplex virus type 2"],"Oral hairy leukoplakia is caused by EBV and signals immunosuppression, typically HIV. Pseudomembranous candidiasis wipes off."],
  ["A pedunculated, cauliflower-like, painless white growth on the soft palate of a 30-year-old is most likely caused by HPV types:","6 and 11",["16 and 18","2 and 4","13 and 32"],"Squamous papilloma is linked to HPV 6 and 11; 16 is the oncogenic type for oropharyngeal cancer, 2 and 4 cause verruca vulgaris, 13 and 32 cause multifocal epithelial hyperplasia."],
  ["Which of the following best explains the rise in oropharyngeal cancer in younger non-smokers?","Persistent infection with high-risk HPV type 16",["Chronic candidal infection","Reactivation of latent EBV","Alcohol use alone"],"HPV-16 integrates and inactivates p53 and Rb pathways in tonsillar and tongue-base epithelium; the HPV vaccine covers this type. EBV is tied to nasopharyngeal, not oropharyngeal, carcinoma."],
  ["A dental assistant sustains a needlestick from a patient of unknown status. Of the blood-borne viruses, the one with the highest risk of transmission per injury is:","Hepatitis B virus",["Hepatitis C virus","Human immunodeficiency virus","Cytomegalovirus"],"HBV is the most transmissible by far (about 6 to 30% in a non-immune worker); HCV and HIV carry much lower per-injury risks, each about 2% or less. This is why HBV vaccination is strongly recommended for dental staff."],
  ["The feature that makes hepatitis B virus such a concern for surface disinfection in the dental operatory is that it:","Remains infectious on dry surfaces for at least a week",["Is airborne","Is destroyed by soap and water alone","Requires mucosal contact to spread"],"HBV survives in dried blood for at least a week, so contaminated surfaces and instruments can transmit it; this drives surface barrier and disinfection protocols."],
  ["A 16-year-old with a sore throat, fatigue and cervical lymphadenopathy develops a widespread rash after being prescribed ampicillin. The most likely underlying infection is:","Epstein-Barr virus",["Group A streptococcus","Cytomegalovirus","Mumps virus"],"Infectious mononucleosis classically produces a rash when ampicillin or amoxicillin is given; streptococcal pharyngitis does not."],
  ["A 7-year-old presents with fever and small vesicles confined to the soft palate and tonsillar pillars, with normal gingivae. The most likely cause is:","Coxsackie A virus (herpangina)",["Herpes simplex virus type 1","Varicella-zoster virus","Measles virus"],"Herpangina is limited to the posterior mouth and spares the gingiva, unlike primary HSV. Measles gives Koplik spots on the buccal mucosa."],
  ["An unvaccinated 9-year-old develops painful bilateral swelling in front of and below both ears with fever. The most likely diagnosis is:","Mumps parotitis",["Acute bacterial sialadenitis","Sjögren's syndrome","Dental abscess of 5.5"],"Bilateral parotid swelling with fever in an unvaccinated child is mumps. Bacterial sialadenitis is usually unilateral with pus from the duct."],
  ["Which oral finding is most strongly associated with HIV infection?","Oral hairy leukoplakia",["Geographic tongue","Recurrent aphthous ulcers","Fordyce granules"],"Hairy leukoplakia, Kaposi sarcoma, necrotising periodontitis and candidiasis are the classic HIV markers; the distractors are common and benign in healthy people."],
  ["Acyclovir works selectively on herpes-infected cells because:","Viral thymidine kinase activates the drug",["It binds the viral envelope","It blocks the host ribosome","It dissolves the viral capsid"],"The virus's own enzyme phosphorylates acyclovir, which then inhibits viral DNA polymerase; uninfected cells barely activate it."]
